# Developing TADp

This compatibility guide now points installed projects to their operating
guides. Detailed contributor procedures belong to the TADp source repository's
repository documentation. That optional context is not required to install or
operate this project. Copying framework reports or maintenance prompts does
not create project history, adoption, acceptance or maintenance authority.

The original headings remain so existing links continue to resolve. These
explanations are non-authoritative; the
[governing suite](../../evidence/authoritative/LLM/00_Codex_Invariant_Suite_Index.md)
owns the rules.

## Change classification

For ordinary project work, start with [getting started](getting-started.md),
[architecture and authority](architecture-and-authority.md) and
[initiative operation](initiative-workflow.md). Generic framework changes need
separately scoped source-repository maintenance authority.

## Before editing

Read the installed project instructions and applicable suite, inspect Git,
identify the exact project boundary, and preserve unrelated work. See
[Git and Codex requirements](git-and-codex-requirements.md). A nested project
uses its enclosing Git worktree without inheriting authority over it.

## Coordinated update surfaces

Use the [project layout](repository-layout.md) to find canonical record owners.
Record source provenance and affected consumers. Installation, synchronization
and prospective adoption are distinct; preserve legacy records under their
recorded revision until an authorized migration.

## Template rules

Use the [coverage map](../../template/TEMPLATE-COVERAGE.md) to select record
sources. Specialize destination copies outside the protected library.
The [prerequisite guide](../guides/template-prerequisites-migration.md)
explains evidence-backed preparation without inventing missing facts or gates.

## Protected-source changes

Run the ordinary [integrity checks](validation-and-quality.md#protected-source-workflow).
Unexpected drift is evidence to investigate, not permission to refresh a lock.
Exact reusable-source or invariant changes require explicit human authority;
ordinary project work cannot modify the protected source in place.

## Prompt rules

Installed project-local `prompts/` is distinct from the protected numbered
workflow in `template/prompts/` and from a source repository's maintenance
prompts. Copying a prompt or changing cwd grants no generic maintenance
authority. Apply the [keyword contract](../../evidence/authoritative/LLM/11_Codex_Prompt_Keyword_Specification.md)
to every task; referenced examples remain inert.

## Git workflow for TADp changes

Project execution follows its actual scope and branch assignments, captured
bases and traceable commits. See [scope operation](../guides/version-scope-migration.md)
and [Git requirements](git-and-codex-requirements.md). A source repository's
branch names and contributor procedures are not assignments for this project.

## Verification checklist

Use [validation and quality](validation-and-quality.md) for configured-interpreter
commands, structural checks, regressions and limitations. Run the project's
own product checks as required by its accepted work. Passing a structural
check supplies neither human acceptance nor proof of product correctness.

## Releasing a protocol revision

Source-repository publication and release are separately authorized operations.
An installed project records its actual governing revision and adoption
authority; copied files, validation or initiative completion establish no
protocol release. Preserve the earlier baseline when adopting a revision,
following the applicable [migration guides](../README.md#operating-guides).
